    "evidence_text": "In this paper, we propose to build a general knowledge learning engine for chatbots to enable them to continuously and interactively learn new knowledge during conversations.\n\nAs time goes by, they become more and more knowledgeable and better and better at learning and conversation.\n\nLiLi works by imitating how humans acquire knowledge and perform inference during an interactive conversation.\n\nIntroduction\tChatbots such as dialog and question-answering systems have a long history in AI and natural language processing.\n\nRecent neural conversation models  ,  ,  are even able to perform open-ended conversations.\n\nHowever, since they\n\ndo not use explicit knowledge bases and do not perform inference, they often suffer from generic and dull responses  ,  .\n\nMore recently,  and  proposed to use knowledge bases (KBs) to help generate responses for knowledge-grounded conversation.\n\nHowever, one major weakness of all existing chat systems is that they do not explicitly or implicitly learn new knowledge in the conversation process.\n\nThis seriously limits the scope of their applications.\n\nIn contrast, we humans constantly learn new knowledge in our conversations.\n\nIt is thus important for a chatbot to continuously learn new knowledge in the conversation process to expand its KB and to improve its conversation ability.\n\nWe call the new problem open-world knowledge base completion (OKBC).\n\nOKBC generalizes KBC.\n\nBelow, we show that solving OKBC naturally provides the ground for knowledge learning and inference in conversations.\n\nQueries are facts whose truth values need to be determined.\n\nNote that we do not study fact or\n\nus to communicate better in the future.\n\nWe call this lifelong interactive learning and inference (LiLi).\n\nC-PR enumerates paths by performing\n\nRelated Work\tTo the best of our knowledge, we are not aware of any knowledge learning system that can learn new knowledge in the conversation process.\n\nThis section thus discusses other related work.\n\nAmong existing KB completion approaches,  extended the vector space model for zero-shot KB inference.\n\nRecently,  proposed a method using external text corpus to perform inference on unknown entities.\n\nHowever, the method cannot handle unknown relations.\n\nNone of the existing KB inference methods perform interactive knowledge learning like LiLi.\n\nNELL  continuously updates its KB using facts extracted from the Web.\n\nWe focus on user interactions in this paper.\n\nOur work is related to interactive language learning (ILL)  ,  , but these are not about KB completion.\n\nThe work in  allows a learner to ask questions in dialogue.\n\nHowever, this work used RL to learn about whether to ask the user or not.\n\nThe \u201cwhat to ask aspect\" was manually designed by modeling synthetic\n\ntasks.\n\nLiLi formulates query-specific inference strategies which embed interaction behaviors.\n\nMore details about lifelong learning can be found in the book  .\n\nThe framework improves its performance over time through user interaction and knowledge retention.\n\nMapping open-world to close-world.\n\nBeing\n\n, INLINEFORM8 , INLINEFORM9 , INLINEFORM10 .\n\nWe used Keras deep learning library to implement and train the prediction model.\n\nWe set batch-size as 128, max.\n\nAdam optimizer and early stopping were used in training.\n\nLabeled Dataset Generation and Simulated User Creation.\n\nWe create a simulated user for each KB to evaluate LiLi.\n\nWe followed  for labeled dataset generation.\n\nFor Freebase, we found 86 relations with\n\nEvaluation-III: User Interaction vs. Performance.\n\nLiLi\u2019s performance improves significantly as it acquires more clues from the user.\n\nAnswering MLQ\u2019s and CLQ\u2019s is very hard\n\nfor simulated users (unlike crowd-sourcing) as often INLINEFORM5 lacks the required triple.\n\nThe results show a clear trend in overall performance improvement when the user responds.\n\nBut, the analysis shows the effectiveness of LiLi\u2019s guessing mechanism and continual learning ability that help in achieving avg.\n\nConclusion\t In this paper, we are interested in building a generic engine for continuous knowledge learning in human-machine conversations.\n\nWe believe that a system with the LiLi approach can serve as a knowledge learning engine for conversations.\n\nOur future work will improve LiLi to make more accurate.",
